            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Russian for to fight, then the Russian for an enemy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: победа",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "победа (pobéda) — \"a victory, a win\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Победа была очень важной для всех. Pobéda bylá óchen vázhnoy dlya vsekh. The victory was very important for everyone."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine. День Победы — Victory Day, 9 May."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "победа (pobéda) is inherited from Old East Slavic poběda \"victory, defeat\" (EtymDB), built on по- and беда \"misfortune\": first the trouble one side brings on the other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
